## Examples

### Good Examples

```tsx
// Proper CSS Module usage with TypeScript
import styles from './Button.module.css';

interface ButtonProps {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  size?: 'small' | 'medium' | 'large';
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onClick?: () => void;
}

export const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({
  variant = 'primary',
  size = 'medium',
  children,
  onClick
}) => {
  const buttonClass = [
    styles.button,
    styles[`button${variant.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + variant.slice(1)}`],
    styles[`button${size.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + size.slice(1)}`]
  ].join(' ');

  return (
    <button className={buttonClass} onClick={onClick}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};
```

```css
/* Proper CSS Module structure */
.button {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md);
  border: none;
  border-radius: var(--border-radius);
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: all var(--transition-speed) ease;
  font-family: var(--font-family);
  font-size: var(--font-size-base);
}

.buttonPrimary {
  background: var(--color-primary);
  color: white;
}

.buttonPrimary:hover {
  background: var(--color-primary-dark);
}

.buttonSecondary {
  background: var(--color-secondary);
  color: white;
}

.buttonSmall {
  padding: var(--spacing-xs) var(--spacing-sm);
  font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
}

.buttonLarge {
  padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
}
```

### Bad Examples

```tsx
// Global CSS classes - violates scoping
export function BadComponent() {
  return (
    <div className="bad-component">
      <h2 className="title">Title</h2>
      <p className="content">Content</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

*Fails: Uses global CSS classes instead of CSS Modules.*

```css
/* Global CSS - violates component scoping */
.bad-component {
  background: #1a4d5c;
  padding: 20px;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

.title {
  color: white;
  font-size: 24px;
}

.content {
  color: #333;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
```

*Fails: Global CSS classes that can conflict with other components.*

```css
/* Using !important - violates specificity rules */
.button {
  background: var(--color-primary) !important;
  color: white !important;
  padding: 10px 20px !important;
}
```

*Fails: Uses !important declarations instead of proper CSS specificity.*

## Implementation Guidelines

### CSS Module Setup
1. Create component directory with `.tsx` and `.module.css` files
2. Import CSS Module in component file
3. Use semantic class names in CSS Module
4. Apply classes using `styles.className` syntax
5. Use CSS custom properties for consistent values

### Performance Optimization
1. Use `will-change` sparingly and only for elements that will actually change
2. Implement `contain` property for isolated components
3. Use `transform` and `opacity` for animations (GPU-accelerated)
4. Avoid layout thrashing by batching DOM reads/writes
5. Use `content-visibility: auto` for off-screen content

### Accessibility Implementation
1. Always provide visible focus indicators
2. Support `prefers-reduced-motion` media query
3. Ensure proper color contrast ratios
4. Use semantic HTML elements
5. Test with screen readers and keyboard navigation

### Validation and Testing
1. Use TypeScript for CSS Module class validation
2. Implement CSS linting rules
3. Test across different browsers and devices
4. Validate accessibility with automated tools
5. Performance test with Core Web Vitals

